1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:01,540
In 1999,

2
00:00:02,360 --> 00:00:05,000
investors believed the internet was going

3
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:06,160
to change the world.

4
00:00:06,810 --> 00:00:07,510
They were right.

5
00:00:08,374 --> 00:00:10,114
But that didn't stop trillions of

6
00:00:10,114 --> 00:00:12,974
dollars in market value from disappearing.

7
00:00:13,689 --> 00:00:14,749
Companies collapsed.

8
00:00:15,949 --> 00:00:17,089
Investors lost fortunes.

9
00:00:17,789 --> 00:00:19,529
And one of the greatest technological

10
00:00:19,529 --> 00:00:23,269
revolutions in history became associated with

11
00:00:23,269 --> 00:00:25,909
one of its most spectacular financial disasters.

12
00:00:26,813 --> 00:00:28,373
More than two decades later,

13
00:00:28,853 --> 00:00:32,533
another technological revolution is attracting extraordinary

14
00:00:32,533 --> 00:00:33,573
amounts of money.

15
00:00:34,239 --> 00:00:35,679
Artificial intelligence.

16
00:00:36,781 --> 00:00:39,921
Technology giants are constructing enormous data

17
00:00:39,921 --> 00:00:40,541
centres.

18
00:00:41,281 --> 00:00:44,661
Semiconductor companies are selling advanced processors.

19
00:00:45,681 --> 00:00:47,381
Investors are betting on a future

20
00:00:47,381 --> 00:00:51,501
where intelligent machines transform nearly every industry.

21
00:00:52,347 --> 00:00:54,987
But beneath this historic investment boom

22
00:00:55,387 --> 00:00:57,007
lies an uncomfortable question.

23
00:00:57,737 --> 00:01:00,317
Is humanity building its next great

24
00:01:00,317 --> 00:01:01,597
economic revolution,

25
00:01:02,007 --> 00:01:03,577
or repeating one of its most

26
00:01:03,577 --> 00:01:05,537
expensive financial mistakes?

27
00:01:06,307 --> 00:01:07,607
To understand the answer,

28
00:01:08,067 --> 00:01:09,287
we need to travel back to

29
00:01:09,287 --> 00:01:11,107
a time when the internet was

30
00:01:11,107 --> 00:01:11,727
still new.

31
00:01:13,726 --> 00:01:16,246
During the early 1990s,

32
00:01:16,526 --> 00:01:18,466
the internet began to change from

33
00:01:18,466 --> 00:01:21,446
a specialized network into something ordinary

34
00:01:21,446 --> 00:01:22,646
people could use.

35
00:01:23,260 --> 00:01:24,240
For the first time,

36
00:01:24,700 --> 00:01:27,140
businesses could communicate with customers around

37
00:01:27,140 --> 00:01:30,020
the world without opening physical stores

38
00:01:30,020 --> 00:01:31,120
in every country.

39
00:01:31,891 --> 00:01:34,651
Information could travel almost instantly.

40
00:01:35,640 --> 00:01:39,620
Entire industries suddenly appeared vulnerable to disruption.

41
00:01:40,417 --> 00:01:43,937
Entrepreneurs recognized an enormous opportunity.

42
00:01:44,929 --> 00:01:45,869
Online shopping,

43
00:01:46,369 --> 00:01:47,449
digital advertising,

44
00:01:48,129 --> 00:01:51,489
internet banking and electronic communication promised

45
00:01:51,489 --> 00:01:53,969
to change the way economies operated.

46
00:01:54,771 --> 00:01:56,891
Investors wanted to be part of

47
00:01:56,891 --> 00:01:57,311
that future.

48
00:01:58,196 --> 00:02:00,856
Money began flowing into internet companies

49
00:02:00,856 --> 00:02:02,556
at a remarkable pace.

50
00:02:03,256 --> 00:02:05,156
But something unusual happened.

51
00:02:06,018 --> 00:02:08,538
Instead of valuing businesses primarily on

52
00:02:08,538 --> 00:02:09,218
their profits,

53
00:02:10,038 --> 00:02:13,058
investors increasingly valued them on their potential.

54
00:02:13,850 --> 00:02:14,770
Website visitors,

55
00:02:15,430 --> 00:02:17,550
user growth and market share became

56
00:02:17,550 --> 00:02:19,570
important indicators of success,

57
00:02:20,170 --> 00:02:22,170
even when companies were losing money.

58
00:02:22,823 --> 00:02:26,103
Many businesses believed that profitability could

59
00:02:26,103 --> 00:02:26,663
come later.

60
00:02:27,397 --> 00:02:29,717
The immediate priority was expansion.

61
00:02:30,766 --> 00:02:33,626
Imagine launching an online store that

62
00:02:33,626 --> 00:02:35,686
loses money on every order.

63
00:02:36,284 --> 00:02:37,784
Under normal circumstances,

64
00:02:38,564 --> 00:02:40,684
investors would question whether such a

65
00:02:40,684 --> 00:02:41,764
business could survive.

66
00:02:42,518 --> 00:02:43,998
But during the dot-com boom,

67
00:02:44,598 --> 00:02:47,578
rapid expansion could attract additional investment

68
00:02:47,878 --> 00:02:50,698
and push a company's valuation even higher.

69
00:02:51,369 --> 00:02:53,589
The expectation was that once the

70
00:02:53,589 --> 00:02:55,149
company became large enough,

71
00:02:55,689 --> 00:02:57,429
profits would eventually follow.

72
00:02:58,127 --> 00:02:58,667
Sometimes,

73
00:02:58,667 --> 00:03:00,867
that expectation was reasonable.

74
00:03:01,779 --> 00:03:02,299
Sometimes,

75
00:03:02,379 --> 00:03:04,399
it was little more than speculation.

76
00:03:05,306 --> 00:03:07,786
And as stock prices continued rising,

77
00:03:08,366 --> 00:03:11,826
distinguishing between the two became increasingly difficult.

78
00:03:13,867 --> 00:03:15,547
By the beginning of 2000,

79
00:03:16,107 --> 00:03:19,087
enthusiasm for internet companies had reached

80
00:03:19,087 --> 00:03:19,747
fever pitch.

81
00:03:20,449 --> 00:03:21,589
The Nasdaq Composite,

82
00:03:22,049 --> 00:03:24,469
heavily influenced by technology stocks,

83
00:03:25,009 --> 00:03:29,929
had climbed roughly 86% in 1999 alone.

84
00:03:30,627 --> 00:03:32,727
But beneath the rising stock prices,

85
00:03:33,167 --> 00:03:34,687
many businesses were struggling.

86
00:03:35,395 --> 00:03:37,575
They were spending heavily on advertising,

87
00:03:38,215 --> 00:03:41,295
employees and infrastructure without earning enough

88
00:03:41,295 --> 00:03:43,075
revenue to sustain themselves.

89
00:03:43,910 --> 00:03:47,130
Their survival depended on investors continuing

90
00:03:47,130 --> 00:03:48,410
to provide capital.

91
00:03:49,181 --> 00:03:51,581
Then confidence began to disappear.

92
00:03:52,463 --> 00:03:55,063
Investors began to worry about company

93
00:03:55,063 --> 00:03:57,083
valuations and profitability.

94
00:03:58,092 --> 00:03:59,932
Stock prices started falling.

95
00:04:00,902 --> 00:04:03,102
For businesses that depended on external

96
00:04:03,102 --> 00:04:03,922
financing,

97
00:04:04,542 --> 00:04:08,462
declining investor confidence became an existential threat.

98
00:04:09,093 --> 00:04:10,353
Without fresh capital,

99
00:04:10,793 --> 00:04:13,113
they could no longer finance their operations.

100
00:04:14,016 --> 00:04:15,736
Companies began shutting down.

101
00:04:16,356 --> 00:04:17,716
Employees lost their jobs.

102
00:04:18,456 --> 00:04:20,796
Investors watched their savings evaporate.

103
00:04:21,306 --> 00:04:23,426
Between its peak in March 2000

104
00:04:23,726 --> 00:04:25,726
and the subsequent market bottom in

105
00:04:25,726 --> 00:04:26,986
2002,

106
00:04:27,626 --> 00:04:30,746
the Nasdaq lost roughly 78% of

107
00:04:30,746 --> 00:04:31,226
its value.

108
00:04:31,937 --> 00:04:35,457
Imagine investing $10,000 near the peak

109
00:04:35,457 --> 00:04:37,937
in a portfolio that tracked the index.

110
00:04:38,663 --> 00:04:40,603
Ignoring dividends and fees,

111
00:04:41,223 --> 00:04:43,063
that investment would have fallen to

112
00:04:43,063 --> 00:04:44,543
about $2,200.

113
00:04:45,638 --> 00:04:48,458
Yet something remarkable happened after the crash.

114
00:04:49,164 --> 00:04:51,104
The internet continued growing.

115
00:04:52,050 --> 00:04:53,710
Online shopping expanded.

116
00:04:54,650 --> 00:04:56,950
Digital advertising grew in importance.

117
00:04:57,710 --> 00:05:00,650
Businesses built successful models around technologies

118
00:05:00,760 --> 00:05:03,790
that had once attracted excessive speculation.

119
00:05:04,660 --> 00:05:06,560
The internet was not the illusion.

120
00:05:07,552 --> 00:05:11,572
The unrealistic financial expectations surrounding many

121
00:05:11,572 --> 00:05:13,072
internet companies were.

122
00:05:13,646 --> 00:05:16,346
And that distinction is essential to

123
00:05:16,346 --> 00:05:19,046
understanding artificial intelligence today.

124
00:05:21,101 --> 00:05:24,001
The modern AI boom accelerated sharply

125
00:05:24,001 --> 00:05:28,241
once generative AI systems became widely accessible.

126
00:05:28,987 --> 00:05:29,407
Suddenly,

127
00:05:29,407 --> 00:05:31,987
ordinary people could interact with machines

128
00:05:31,987 --> 00:05:34,067
capable of generating text,

129
00:05:34,587 --> 00:05:35,567
writing software,

130
00:05:36,087 --> 00:05:39,327
creating images and assisting with complex tasks.

131
00:05:39,998 --> 00:05:43,138
Businesses began exploring how artificial intelligence

132
00:05:43,138 --> 00:05:44,818
could improve productivity,

133
00:05:45,458 --> 00:05:48,658
reduce costs and create entirely new services.

134
00:05:49,500 --> 00:05:52,940
Technology companies saw a potentially vast market.

135
00:05:53,681 --> 00:05:57,281
But artificial intelligence requires more than software.

136
00:05:57,938 --> 00:06:00,998
Behind every advanced AI system lies

137
00:06:00,998 --> 00:06:02,598
a vast physical infrastructure.

138
00:06:03,464 --> 00:06:07,784
Specialized processors perform enormous numbers of calculations.

139
00:06:08,613 --> 00:06:12,233
Servers operate inside sophisticated data centres.

140
00:06:13,086 --> 00:06:16,386
Cooling systems prevent equipment from overheating.

141
00:06:17,138 --> 00:06:20,238
Electricity networks supply the energy necessary

142
00:06:20,238 --> 00:06:22,498
to keep these facilities running.

143
00:06:23,211 --> 00:06:26,971
Building this infrastructure requires substantial investment.

144
00:06:27,859 --> 00:06:29,459
In November 2026,

145
00:06:29,979 --> 00:06:32,979
the European Central Bank highlighted estimates

146
00:06:32,979 --> 00:06:36,919
suggesting that cumulative capital expenditure by

147
00:06:36,919 --> 00:06:40,279
seven major American technology companies could

148
00:06:40,279 --> 00:06:44,999
reach approximately $5.5 trillion by 2030.

149
00:06:46,136 --> 00:06:47,076
That is a forecast,

150
00:06:47,536 --> 00:06:49,036
not money that has already been

151
00:06:49,036 --> 00:06:49,556
spent,

152
00:06:50,016 --> 00:06:52,256
and it includes broader company investment

153
00:06:52,256 --> 00:06:55,136
rather than exclusively AI expenditure.

154
00:06:56,008 --> 00:06:56,628
Even so,

155
00:06:57,028 --> 00:06:58,588
it shows the sheer scale of

156
00:06:58,588 --> 00:06:59,128
the expansion.

157
00:06:59,988 --> 00:07:02,608
Companies are spending today because they

158
00:07:02,608 --> 00:07:06,008
expect AI services to generate substantial

159
00:07:06,008 --> 00:07:07,708
economic value tomorrow.

160
00:07:08,428 --> 00:07:10,688
But spending money and earning profitable

161
00:07:10,688 --> 00:07:12,868
returns on that money are two

162
00:07:12,868 --> 00:07:13,968
very different things.

163
00:07:16,104 --> 00:07:18,484
To understand the financial risks,

164
00:07:19,204 --> 00:07:22,224
imagine a simplified artificial intelligence economy.

165
00:07:22,926 --> 00:07:27,266
A semiconductor manufacturer produces advanced computing chips.

166
00:07:27,965 --> 00:07:30,545
A cloud computing company purchases those

167
00:07:30,545 --> 00:07:33,285
chips and installs them inside a

168
00:07:33,285 --> 00:07:33,885
data centre.

169
00:07:34,611 --> 00:07:37,751
An AI developer rents computing capacity

170
00:07:37,751 --> 00:07:40,431
from that cloud company to train

171
00:07:40,431 --> 00:07:41,871
and operate its models.

172
00:07:42,652 --> 00:07:43,092
Finally,

173
00:07:43,392 --> 00:07:46,172
businesses and individual customers pay to

174
00:07:46,172 --> 00:07:48,312
use the resulting AI services.

175
00:07:49,141 --> 00:07:50,781
Money moves through the system,

176
00:07:51,181 --> 00:07:53,201
supporting revenue at every stage.

177
00:07:53,984 --> 00:07:55,844
When customer demand expands,

178
00:07:56,384 --> 00:07:58,104
the entire network can benefit.

179
00:07:58,886 --> 00:08:01,486
Chip manufacturers sell more processors.

180
00:08:02,485 --> 00:08:05,485
Cloud companies rent more computing capacity.

181
00:08:06,520 --> 00:08:09,200
AI developers attract more customers.

182
00:08:10,092 --> 00:08:13,212
And investors anticipate higher future profits.

183
00:08:14,181 --> 00:08:16,381
But what happens when businesses within

184
00:08:16,381 --> 00:08:19,681
this ecosystem also finance one another?

185
00:08:20,364 --> 00:08:23,984
Imagine a chip manufacturer investing $1 billion

186
00:08:23,984 --> 00:08:26,044
in an AI developer.

187
00:08:26,738 --> 00:08:28,778
That developer uses part of its

188
00:08:28,778 --> 00:08:31,278
funding to purchase computing services.

189
00:08:32,096 --> 00:08:34,736
The computing provider then purchases more

190
00:08:34,736 --> 00:08:36,916
chips from the original manufacturer.

191
00:08:37,808 --> 00:08:40,048
Each company may benefit from the

192
00:08:40,048 --> 00:08:41,028
resulting activity.

193
00:08:41,886 --> 00:08:43,746
But analysts must ask how much

194
00:08:43,746 --> 00:08:46,966
demand is ultimately supported by paying

195
00:08:46,966 --> 00:08:47,606
customers,

196
00:08:48,006 --> 00:08:50,966
rather than continued financing from within

197
00:08:50,966 --> 00:08:52,086
the industry itself.

198
00:08:52,897 --> 00:08:55,937
Such arrangements do not automatically indicate

199
00:08:55,937 --> 00:08:58,237
fraud or financial manipulation.

200
00:08:59,117 --> 00:09:02,477
Strategic partnerships can help businesses develop

201
00:09:02,477 --> 00:09:03,757
useful technologies.

202
00:09:04,521 --> 00:09:04,821
However,

203
00:09:05,181 --> 00:09:07,421
they make it especially important to

204
00:09:07,421 --> 00:09:10,121
examine the underlying sources of revenue.

205
00:09:10,984 --> 00:09:11,564
In the end,

206
00:09:11,824 --> 00:09:14,564
the entire investment chain must generate

207
00:09:14,564 --> 00:09:17,804
enough external economic value to support

208
00:09:17,804 --> 00:09:19,084
the money being invested.

209
00:09:19,855 --> 00:09:20,295
Otherwise,

210
00:09:20,655 --> 00:09:23,555
the system becomes increasingly dependent on

211
00:09:23,555 --> 00:09:25,555
expectations about the future.

212
00:09:26,316 --> 00:09:29,496
And expectations can change frighteningly quickly.

213
00:09:31,670 --> 00:09:34,230
Another important development is the way

214
00:09:34,230 --> 00:09:36,930
AI infrastructure is being financed.

215
00:09:37,766 --> 00:09:40,646
Imagine a technology company that generates

216
00:09:40,646 --> 00:09:43,866
$10 billion in operating cash flow.

217
00:09:44,514 --> 00:09:47,134
After spending $6 billion on buildings,

218
00:09:47,614 --> 00:09:49,694
equipment and other capital investments,

219
00:09:50,274 --> 00:09:52,434
it has $4 billion left over:

220
00:09:52,894 --> 00:09:55,674
a simplified measure of free cash flow.

221
00:09:56,348 --> 00:09:59,588
Now imagine its investment requirements rise

222
00:09:59,588 --> 00:10:02,708
to $14 billion while operating cash

223
00:10:02,708 --> 00:10:04,348
flow remains unchanged.

224
00:10:05,224 --> 00:10:07,524
Its free cash flow becomes negative.

225
00:10:08,508 --> 00:10:10,428
The company must use existing cash

226
00:10:10,428 --> 00:10:10,988
reserves,

227
00:10:11,448 --> 00:10:12,268
sell assets,

228
00:10:12,688 --> 00:10:15,128
raise equity or borrow money to

229
00:10:15,128 --> 00:10:15,908
cover the difference.

230
00:10:16,658 --> 00:10:18,278
In October 2026,

231
00:10:18,778 --> 00:10:21,658
the International Monetary Fund highlighted the

232
00:10:21,658 --> 00:10:23,658
growing use of debt across the

233
00:10:23,658 --> 00:10:26,998
AI ecosystem and warned that disappointing

234
00:10:26,998 --> 00:10:30,378
returns could place additional pressure on

235
00:10:30,378 --> 00:10:31,618
financing arrangements.

236
00:10:32,449 --> 00:10:35,369
This matters because debt creates obligations

237
00:10:35,449 --> 00:10:38,309
that do not disappear simply because

238
00:10:38,309 --> 00:10:40,149
customer demand weakens.

239
00:10:40,865 --> 00:10:42,825
Lenders still expect repayment.

240
00:10:43,781 --> 00:10:46,021
Interest payments must still be made.

241
00:10:46,842 --> 00:10:49,862
And long-term financial commitments can remain

242
00:10:49,862 --> 00:10:53,302
in place even when technology becomes outdated.

243
00:10:54,102 --> 00:10:56,802
Consider a data centre financed with

244
00:10:56,802 --> 00:10:57,922
a long-term loan.

245
00:10:58,595 --> 00:11:00,975
If newer processors make its equipment

246
00:11:00,975 --> 00:11:03,955
less competitive before the investment has

247
00:11:03,955 --> 00:11:05,695
earned back its original cost,

248
00:11:06,295 --> 00:11:08,735
the owner could face financial difficulties.

249
00:11:09,517 --> 00:11:11,517
The technology may have improved.

250
00:11:12,436 --> 00:11:14,456
But the economics of the original

251
00:11:14,456 --> 00:11:16,636
investment may have deteriorated.

252
00:11:17,672 --> 00:11:20,392
This creates a fundamental tension between

253
00:11:20,392 --> 00:11:23,352
the rapid evolution of artificial intelligence

254
00:11:23,642 --> 00:11:26,692
and the financial commitments required to

255
00:11:26,692 --> 00:11:27,752
build its infrastructure.

256
00:11:29,983 --> 00:11:32,123
The answer depends on how we

257
00:11:32,123 --> 00:11:33,683
measure the size of a bubble.

258
00:11:34,396 --> 00:11:36,236
Are we measuring the amount of

259
00:11:36,236 --> 00:11:37,896
infrastructure being built?

260
00:11:38,570 --> 00:11:41,170
The market value of technology companies?

261
00:11:42,149 --> 00:11:44,389
The amount of debt supporting the expansion?

262
00:11:45,283 --> 00:11:47,863
Or the difference between company valuations

263
00:11:48,083 --> 00:11:50,623
and their underlying economic value?

264
00:11:51,275 --> 00:11:54,195
These measurements can produce very different answers.

265
00:11:54,976 --> 00:11:57,156
Research cited by the European Central

266
00:11:57,156 --> 00:12:01,176
Bank in November 2026 estimated that

267
00:12:01,176 --> 00:12:04,456
AI investment needs could exceed 3%

268
00:12:04,456 --> 00:12:07,236
of American economic output each year

269
00:12:07,236 --> 00:12:11,836
from 2025 to 2032.

270
00:12:12,635 --> 00:12:13,275
For comparison,

271
00:12:13,875 --> 00:12:17,455
the internet boom between 1996 and

272
00:12:17,455 --> 00:12:21,655
2003 took up about 1.1%.

273
00:12:22,330 --> 00:12:24,570
These are not perfectly equivalent historical

274
00:12:24,570 --> 00:12:25,310
measurements,

275
00:12:25,750 --> 00:12:28,230
and the AI figures depend on forecasts.

276
00:12:29,085 --> 00:12:29,425
Nevertheless,

277
00:12:30,145 --> 00:12:33,185
they suggest that today's infrastructure expansion

278
00:12:33,185 --> 00:12:36,445
could be considerably larger relative to

279
00:12:36,445 --> 00:12:36,905
the economy.

280
00:12:37,671 --> 00:12:40,491
But larger investment does not automatically

281
00:12:40,491 --> 00:12:42,791
mean a larger financial bubble.

282
00:12:43,461 --> 00:12:46,161
A railway connecting productive cities may

283
00:12:46,161 --> 00:12:49,681
require enormous investment and still generate

284
00:12:49,681 --> 00:12:51,721
substantial long-term returns.

285
00:12:52,565 --> 00:12:55,485
An expensive railway connecting places with

286
00:12:55,485 --> 00:12:59,145
insufficient demand may become a financial disaster.

287
00:12:59,951 --> 00:13:02,131
The critical question is not simply

288
00:13:02,131 --> 00:13:04,071
how much money is being spent.

289
00:13:04,785 --> 00:13:06,765
It is whether the economic returns

290
00:13:06,765 --> 00:13:08,685
will justify that spending.

291
00:13:10,784 --> 00:13:13,344
There are several important differences between

292
00:13:13,344 --> 00:13:15,384
the dot-com era and the current

293
00:13:15,384 --> 00:13:16,344
AI boom.

294
00:13:17,025 --> 00:13:19,325
Many of the internet startups that

295
00:13:19,325 --> 00:13:22,405
attracted sky-high valuations during the late

296
00:13:22,405 --> 00:13:26,525
1990s had weak business models and

297
00:13:26,525 --> 00:13:28,245
limited financial resources.

298
00:13:28,957 --> 00:13:29,297
Today,

299
00:13:29,637 --> 00:13:32,077
several of the largest companies investing

300
00:13:32,077 --> 00:13:35,617
in artificial intelligence already operate highly

301
00:13:35,617 --> 00:13:36,657
profitable businesses.

302
00:13:37,513 --> 00:13:39,853
They generate revenue from cloud computing,

303
00:13:40,513 --> 00:13:40,953
advertising,

304
00:13:41,613 --> 00:13:41,993
software,

305
00:13:42,473 --> 00:13:44,353
subscriptions and digital commerce.

306
00:13:45,142 --> 00:13:47,462
This gives them resources that many

307
00:13:47,462 --> 00:13:50,422
speculative dot-com companies never possessed.

308
00:13:51,169 --> 00:13:54,789
Artificial intelligence also has practical applications

309
00:13:55,049 --> 00:13:57,509
that extend beyond future promises.

310
00:13:58,307 --> 00:14:00,707
Businesses are already using AI systems

311
00:14:00,707 --> 00:14:02,847
to assist with software development,

312
00:14:03,507 --> 00:14:04,407
customer support,

313
00:14:04,827 --> 00:14:06,967
research and administrative work.

314
00:14:07,587 --> 00:14:11,467
These applications can create genuine economic benefits.

315
00:14:12,206 --> 00:14:12,646
However,

316
00:14:13,246 --> 00:14:16,646
profitable companies can still make unprofitable investments.

317
00:14:17,489 --> 00:14:19,749
And a useful technology does not

318
00:14:19,749 --> 00:14:22,789
guarantee attractive returns for every company

319
00:14:22,789 --> 00:14:24,809
involved in developing it.

320
00:14:25,357 --> 00:14:26,537
During the internet boom,

321
00:14:27,117 --> 00:14:30,837
businesses constructed extensive fibre-optic networks to

322
00:14:30,837 --> 00:14:32,817
prepare for future internet traffic.

323
00:14:33,436 --> 00:14:37,256
Some infrastructure investments were financially unsuccessful

324
00:14:37,746 --> 00:14:41,196
because demand developed more slowly than expected.

325
00:14:41,988 --> 00:14:44,788
Yet those networks eventually helped support

326
00:14:44,788 --> 00:14:47,308
the expansion of the digital economy.

327
00:14:48,016 --> 00:14:50,476
It is entirely possible that artificial

328
00:14:50,476 --> 00:14:53,316
intelligence follows a similar pattern.

329
00:14:54,012 --> 00:14:57,112
Society could benefit enormously from infrastructure

330
00:14:57,482 --> 00:15:01,412
that produces disappointing financial returns for

331
00:15:01,412 --> 00:15:03,072
some of its original investors.

332
00:15:03,990 --> 00:15:07,470
Technological success and investment success are

333
00:15:07,470 --> 00:15:09,550
not necessarily the same thing.

334
00:15:11,580 --> 00:15:14,720
Imagine that AI adoption continues growing,

335
00:15:15,160 --> 00:15:17,420
but customers are unwilling to pay

336
00:15:17,420 --> 00:15:21,260
enough to justify the industry's enormous investments.

337
00:15:22,100 --> 00:15:25,560
Cloud companies may reduce their expansion plans.

338
00:15:26,294 --> 00:15:30,034
Semiconductor manufacturers may receive fewer orders.

339
00:15:30,894 --> 00:15:33,634
Data centre developers may struggle to

340
00:15:33,634 --> 00:15:34,354
attract tenants.

341
00:15:35,126 --> 00:15:38,926
Businesses with substantial financial obligations could

342
00:15:38,926 --> 00:15:40,346
face mounting pressure.

343
00:15:41,051 --> 00:15:44,411
Investors would begin revising their expectations.

344
00:15:45,554 --> 00:15:48,494
And because financial markets anticipate future

345
00:15:48,494 --> 00:15:49,154
earnings,

346
00:15:49,774 --> 00:15:52,234
company valuations could fall before the

347
00:15:52,234 --> 00:15:55,314
full economic consequences become visible.

348
00:15:56,049 --> 00:15:59,209
The effects might extend beyond technology companies.

349
00:16:00,090 --> 00:16:01,210
Retirement funds,

350
00:16:01,730 --> 00:16:05,670
investment portfolios and financial institutions hold

351
00:16:05,670 --> 00:16:08,370
exposure to technology-related assets.

352
00:16:09,124 --> 00:16:10,284
Construction companies,

353
00:16:10,984 --> 00:16:14,624
electricity providers and equipment manufacturers may

354
00:16:14,624 --> 00:16:17,824
also depend on continued infrastructure expansion.

355
00:16:18,684 --> 00:16:20,644
But a collapse is not inevitable.

356
00:16:21,651 --> 00:16:24,731
Demand could grow sufficiently to justify

357
00:16:24,731 --> 00:16:25,511
the investments.

358
00:16:26,359 --> 00:16:29,979
Technological improvements could reduce operating costs.

359
00:16:30,930 --> 00:16:33,870
New applications could create revenue streams

360
00:16:34,050 --> 00:16:36,530
that are difficult to anticipate today.

361
00:16:37,232 --> 00:16:39,892
The investment boom could also experience

362
00:16:39,892 --> 00:16:42,552
a gradual adjustment rather than a

363
00:16:42,552 --> 00:16:43,372
dramatic crash.

364
00:16:44,082 --> 00:16:46,282
The future depends on how these

365
00:16:46,282 --> 00:16:47,942
competing forces develop.

366
00:16:50,015 --> 00:16:53,015
The dot-com bubble demonstrated that investors

367
00:16:53,015 --> 00:16:56,515
can correctly identify a revolutionary technology

368
00:16:56,515 --> 00:17:00,295
and still misunderstand its financial value.

369
00:17:00,907 --> 00:17:02,647
The internet changed the world.

370
00:17:03,524 --> 00:17:06,124
But not every internet company deserved

371
00:17:06,124 --> 00:17:06,924
its valuation.

372
00:17:07,817 --> 00:17:11,237
Artificial intelligence may also transform the

373
00:17:11,237 --> 00:17:12,097
global economy.

374
00:17:12,839 --> 00:17:14,139
It could reshape industries,

375
00:17:14,639 --> 00:17:17,759
create new businesses and fundamentally alter

376
00:17:17,759 --> 00:17:18,779
how people work.

377
00:17:19,411 --> 00:17:21,371
Yet none of that eliminates the

378
00:17:21,371 --> 00:17:23,691
possibility of excessive speculation,

379
00:17:23,691 --> 00:17:26,871
overinvestment or disappointing returns.

380
00:17:27,702 --> 00:17:30,002
The most important question is not

381
00:17:30,002 --> 00:17:32,622
whether artificial intelligence is useful.

382
00:17:33,401 --> 00:17:35,221
It is whether the profits generated

383
00:17:35,221 --> 00:17:39,061
by artificial intelligence will ultimately justify

384
00:17:39,061 --> 00:17:42,861
the extraordinary financial commitments being made today.

385
00:17:43,565 --> 00:17:45,605
History does not tell us exactly

386
00:17:45,605 --> 00:17:47,085
what will happen next.

387
00:17:47,761 --> 00:17:49,501
But it reminds us that even

388
00:17:49,501 --> 00:17:51,321
the most convincing vision of the

389
00:17:51,321 --> 00:17:54,621
future can become dangerous when financial

390
00:17:54,621 --> 00:17:57,761
expectations move too far ahead of

391
00:17:57,761 --> 00:17:58,721
economic reality.

392
00:17:59,538 --> 00:18:02,438
The dot-com generation learned that lesson

393
00:18:02,438 --> 00:18:03,338
the hard way.

394
00:18:03,964 --> 00:18:06,924
Whether the artificial intelligence generation will

395
00:18:06,924 --> 00:18:09,264
learn it differently remains one of

396
00:18:09,264 --> 00:18:12,064
the most important financial questions of

397
00:18:12,064 --> 00:18:12,584
our time.

398
00:18:13,250 --> 00:18:15,190
This is The Epoch Ledger.

399
00:18:15,892 --> 00:18:17,252
Understanding the past.

400
00:18:17,652 --> 00:18:18,572
Decoding the present.

